          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Reference tracking, prefilters and control constraints",
          "content": "# Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Reference tracking, prefilters and control constraints\n\n**Unit:** Optimal control and quadratic design. **Prerequisites:** Book 11 feedback and state-space fundamentals; preceding lessons in this unit. **Learning objectives:** explain the model, reproduce the worked case, identify an important failure condition, and independently solve the problems.\n\n## Concept and mechanism\n\nA regulator drives a state toward zero; tracking a nonzero command requires feedforward, an error-integrator augmentation or another reference strategy. A prefilter can set the equilibrium gain for an accurate model, but uncertainty and disturbances can leave an offset. State penalties and effort penalties must be assessed alongside hard amplitude and slew constraints. Output tracking does not justify ignoring the actuator signal that causes it.\n\n**Governing relation:** `For x_dot=-a x+b u, u=-Kx+Nr; choose N=(a+bK)/b for unity constant-reference gain`\n\nTo interpret this expression, mark each symbol as a state, command, observation, uncertainty, noise statistic or design weight. Determine whether the result is continuous-time, sampled-time, a static mapping, or a bound. The statement may require nonsingular matrices, bounded variation, a linear model or known timing. List the specific hypotheses before drawing an engineering conclusion.\n\n## Worked example\n\nTake a=2 s^-1, b=1, K=3. The closed-loop plant becomes x_dot=\u22125x+Nr. Unity steady tracking requires N=5, giving x_ss=(5/5)r=r. If the actuator cap is \u00b12 and r=1, at initial x=0 the prefilter requests u=5, so the ideal predicted initial response is not feasible.\n\n**Independent check:** Write the same example as an equation whose two sides must agree after substitution. Check the sign and limiting cases; then change only one number and predict whether the outcome increases, decreases, or requires a full recalculation. Do not mistake numerical agreement under synthetic data for physical validation. A result describing a covariance is conditional on the noise model; one describing stability is conditional on the model and its defined perturbations; one describing an optimum is conditional on both the objective and the feasible set.\n\n## Interpretation and decision\n\nPrefilters work under the assumed static plant. Their lack of integral adaptation means that slow drift in a or b can create steady error. An augmented integral state changes system order and must be checked for controllability and closed-loop stability.\n\nThe design choice should identify the goal being improved\u2014tracking, robustness, estimation, control effort or timing\u2014and a quantity that may become worse. Distinguish a sufficient theorem condition from a necessary condition. For optimization explicitly report actuator limits; for estimation report which measurements support the inference. A calculated number is useful only when its assumptions and units are available to the reader.\n\n## When the argument fails\n\nSetting the reference high enough can saturate an actuator, changing the feedback law from the Riccati design. LQR stability proofs for the ideal unconstrained linear loop cannot simply be applied after hard clipping.\n\nDescribe a falsifiable counterexample by naming an omitted dynamic, a violated constraint, an unobservable mode or a mischaracterized uncertainty set. Indicate which measured variable or computed check would reveal the discrepancy. Repeating the original calculation at the same nominal point cannot test a modeling assumption that never appeared in the equation.\n\n## Engineering case \u2014 Actuated environmental chamber\n\nA model predicts temperature from heater power and measured wall temperature. A quadratic tracking penalty must be balanced against heater power and its rate of change, but power and temperature possess different units. Scale the weights and report a real input constraint. If the heater reaches its ceiling during warm-up, the unconstrained optimal controller is no longer the implemented law. An experiment can compare energy use, settling and temperature overshoot on the same synthetic disturbance trace.\n\n**Lab protocol: Cost accounting.** Construct a table with physical units of each state and input; then show how the selected Q and R weights make their quadratic contributions comparable. Repeat at a second scale or command magnitude. If the optimum moves after simply changing reporting units, the weight transformation was inconsistent.\n\nCreate an assumption ledger listing sensor availability, actuator limits, initial state, operating point and the variable intentionally changed. For the main claim, separate what follows algebraically from what would require a second independent experiment. If the proposed experiment is unable to distinguish competing hypotheses, redesign it before collecting more repeated data.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. **Calculation:** For a=3, b=2 and K=1, calculate the prefilter N. For r=2, x0=0 and |u|\u22643, determine whether the initial input is feasible. Show the intermediate arithmetic, a dimensions or sign check, and the condition under which the result is valid.\n2. **Alternative model:** Construct an alternative interpretation of the same nominal observation, then name one additional signal that would separate the alternatives.\n3. **Sensitivity:** Change one uncertain parameter by 20%. State which original claim remains logically valid, which result needs recalculation, and what new numerical input is required.\n4. **Connection:** Compare the present method to **Finite-horizon quadratic objectives**. Name an assumption they share and one kind of answer each method cannot produce alone.\n5. **Verification:** Draft a five-line test specification listing input, expected outcome, numeric tolerance, potential failure and the reason a passing simulation would not establish hardware safety.\n\n## Reference guidance\n\nA complete answer to the calculation question begins from the displayed governing relation and shows substitutions. The alternative-model answer must predict a different outcome in a specified observation. The sensitivity answer must distinguish parameter error from measurement noise. The connection question should identify a difference in mathematical object or guarantee, not just a difference in terminology. A suitable test reports reproducible settings and a clear fail condition. Use the accompanying unit assessment for explicit numerical checks and worked answer guidance.\n"
