          "id": "sec-4-5",
          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Full-State Feedback Controller Design and Pole Placement via Ackermann",
          "content": "# Unit 4 applied project \u2014 MPC feasibility and receding horizon comparison\n\n## Engineering question\n\nUse x_{k+1}=0.9x_k+0.1u_k, x0=2, |u|\u22641 and reference zero. Derive the one-step quadratic optimum for two penalties and demonstrate a target or terminal constraint that is infeasible. Compare a full open-loop two-step command with first-step-only receding-horizon behavior after an injected disturbance. Record objective, bound satisfaction and fallback rule.\n\nThis is an offline design and evidence exercise. Its output is a reproducible mathematical study and a bounded conclusion, not permission to commission a physical controller. Write the study question in a sentence that could be falsified by a measured trajectory or a computed counterexample. Explain why the chosen input variation contains more information than an arbitrary random curve.\n\n## Setup, model and data protocol\n\nSpecify each variable, unit and initial state. The baseline model must be documented independently of the controller. List any linearization point, noise covariance, uncertainty interval, constraints or bounded disturbance. If the project contains a numerical experiment, provide either a short script or a CSV data table, and log the equation and numerical integration interval that produced it. Avoid a test in which the same output is used both to choose every model parameter and to certify accuracy. If this project cannot be evaluated without a missing sensor or unsupported assumption, make the gap an explicit result.\n\n## Required analytical work\n\n1. Derive the governing state equation, transfer equation, cost, covariance update or stability inequality appropriate to this unit. Where possible, independently derive a special case without a library solver.\n2. Calculate a baseline numerical value and check it using a distinct method: direct substitution, a matrix identity, a simulated recurrence or an analytically known limit. Include the expected dimensions.\n3. Declare a testable range for one key parameter. Sample several values and describe what is and is not proven by that sample. For a mathematical worst-case guarantee, state why the chosen analytic bound applies to the full nominated uncertainty set.\n4. Check performance and feasibility separately. A low cost does not imply constraints are met; a stable trajectory does not prove that an actuator command is allowed. Where the optimizer or model predicts an impossible demand, report infeasibility plainly.\n5. Record at least one deliberately adverse scenario: sensor bias, delayed packet, perturbation outside the fit range, insufficient excitation, a saturated actuator, or a change in the model\u2019s region of validity.\n\n## Measurement and critical interpretation\n\n**Feasibility audit:** Write all dynamic equations and hard limits before minimizing the cost. Solve one tiny case by hand. Then create an impossible target on purpose and insist that the optimizer reports infeasible instead of pretending the output was achieved.\n\nA technically useful report separates mathematical truths under explicit assumptions, simulations of those assumptions, and empirical claims that require physical evidence. Explain which additional variable would most improve confidence. A compelling graph should show labeled axes and a model-versus-reference comparison, not just a beautiful output trace. If a proposed controller improves response time but worsens effort, the tradeoff belongs in the conclusion. Do not discard a failed scenario merely because it disrupts the nominal result.\n\n## Required report package\n\n- Model card: signals and dimensions, operating range, nominal and uncertain parameters, initial state and constraints.\n- One derivation checked by a second independent calculation, with all rounding disclosed.\n- A baseline numeric table and an adverse-case numeric table, each with reproducible values.\n- At least two plots with captions explaining what the curves demonstrate and what they do not.\n- A decision paragraph about the next cheapest test that could reject an important assumption.\n- A separate limitations note identifying implementation, sensor, timing, actuator and safety evidence not supplied by this exercise.\n\n## Assessment rubric\n\n**25% analytical correctness** means the mathematical object and its conditions are identified precisely. **20% numerical traceability** means the results can be recomputed from the stated data. **20% comparison** requires a meaningful alternative, sensitivity or failure scenario. **20% engineering interpretation** requires an explicit distinction between a passed model test and an unverified deployment assumption. **15% communication** covers units, graph labels, repeatability and clear identification of the limits of conclusions. Strong work may conclude that a proposed design is not justified by the evidence; that is not a failure of the assignment.\n"
